1
00:01:35,162 --> 00:01:36,323
What are you doing?

2
00:01:38,131 --> 00:01:40,032
Wiping off my shoe.

3
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:42,795
Did you ever see him sing
before 8:30 in the morning?

4
00:01:42,970 --> 00:01:45,405
Uh-uh. He's also
wearing a new suit.

5
00:01:45,672 --> 00:01:46,765
How can you tell?

6
00:01:46,940 --> 00:01:49,910
He's still got the tag on it.

7
00:01:50,677 --> 00:01:53,875
Ben, you're wearing cologne.

8
00:01:54,715 --> 00:01:56,081
Oh, no, no.

9
00:01:56,249 --> 00:01:58,445
I spilled aftershave
on my shirt.

10
00:01:58,619 --> 00:01:59,643
Bye-bye.

11
00:01:59,820 --> 00:02:01,755
- Oh-ho. Where you going?
- Law seminar.

12
00:02:02,189 --> 00:02:04,954
- Since when do you go to seminars?
- Never too old to learn.

13
00:02:05,125 --> 00:02:07,117
- Well, I'll go with you.
- Too late.

14
00:02:23,877 --> 00:02:25,539
Hello, Ben.

15
00:02:27,914 --> 00:02:29,439
Ladies and gentlemen.

16
00:02:29,616 --> 00:02:31,608
Ladies and gentlemen.

17
00:02:31,785 --> 00:02:35,017
It is indeed a delight to
welcome back to Atlanta

18
00:02:35,188 --> 00:02:38,158
one of the most successful
assistant district attorneys

19
00:02:38,325 --> 00:02:39,918
this city has ever had.

20
00:02:40,494 --> 00:02:43,931
All the way from Los Angeles,
where she currently makes her home,

21
00:02:44,097 --> 00:02:47,261
please welcome our
guest speaker, Julie March.

22
00:02:59,479 --> 00:03:01,778
Gentlemen, if you'll look
this way, please. Thank you.

23
00:03:01,948 --> 00:03:05,009
Thank you so much. Great
speech. Great speech. Thank you.

24
00:03:05,185 --> 00:03:06,813
Ben.

25
00:03:06,987 --> 00:03:09,183
It was sweet of you to come.

26
00:03:09,356 --> 00:03:11,188
- Hi, Julie.
- Oh.

27
00:03:11,925 --> 00:03:14,622
Hi. Oh, you look good.

28
00:03:14,795 --> 00:03:16,457
- No, you look good.
- Oh...

29
00:03:16,630 --> 00:03:19,122
- How's Los Angeles?
- Not bad.

30
00:03:19,299 --> 00:03:23,168
I mean, what's a few
fires, floods, earthquakes?

31
00:03:23,336 --> 00:03:25,896
Does that mean you've
given up the prosecuting game

32
00:03:26,073 --> 00:03:29,635
- and you're on the lecture circuit?
- No, not me.

33
00:03:29,810 --> 00:03:33,338
I just couldn't pass up the
chance to come back to Atlanta.

34
00:03:33,513 --> 00:03:38,918
See a few old friends
and get paid for it to boot.

35
00:03:40,287 --> 00:03:42,085
I'm flattered you came.

36
00:03:42,255 --> 00:03:44,815
Oh, I'd come to hear
you read the dictionary.

37
00:03:44,991 --> 00:03:46,516
- Miss March, Mr. Matlock.
- One sec.

38
00:03:46,693 --> 00:03:48,053
How about one
with the both of you?

39
00:03:50,530 --> 00:03:52,055
And just one more.

40
00:03:56,737 --> 00:03:59,639
- Here he comes.
- Leave it here so he can see it.

41
00:04:06,713 --> 00:04:08,443
- Morning.
- Morning.

42
00:04:08,615 --> 00:04:10,846
- How was the seminar?
- Fine.

43
00:04:11,017 --> 00:04:14,545
- You learn anything?
- Oh, yeah.

44
00:04:20,594 --> 00:04:22,790
- Funny.
- Ha-ha-ha.

45
00:04:23,063 --> 00:04:27,023
Well, what's the big secret? I don't
know why you just couldn't tell us.

46
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:30,864
Cliff said that you and Julie
used to have a thing for each other.

47
00:04:31,404 --> 00:04:33,999
I never put it so crudely.

48
00:04:34,641 --> 00:04:36,337
We were friends.

49
00:04:36,510 --> 00:04:39,344
Then what's she
doing in Atlanta?

50
00:04:39,513 --> 00:04:41,038
She's here for the seminar.

51
00:04:41,214 --> 00:04:44,207
She would've given that speech in
Poughkeepsie if they had asked her?

52
00:04:46,653 --> 00:04:48,417
She has friends here.

53
00:04:49,089 --> 00:04:52,856
Believe me, living alone for
two years in a strange city...

54
00:04:53,026 --> 00:04:55,086
And L.A. is nothing
if not strange.

55
00:04:55,929 --> 00:04:59,229
That'll force you to get
your priorities straight.

56
00:04:59,399 --> 00:05:04,394
Maybe she's realized
that you are her priority.

57
00:05:21,955 --> 00:05:23,753
Saw your picture in the paper.

58
00:05:23,924 --> 00:05:24,983
You're looking good.

59
00:05:25,158 --> 00:05:27,889
Thanks. I thought
you'd been released.

60
00:05:29,162 --> 00:05:31,461
Yeah, well, I got kind of bored

61
00:05:31,631 --> 00:05:35,295
and I boosted a couple of
cars, got caught, and here I am.

62
00:05:35,468 --> 00:05:38,404
They got this three-time loser
law. It's a real pain in the butt.

63
00:05:38,572 --> 00:05:40,837
Earl, why did you call me?

64
00:05:42,876 --> 00:05:47,541
They just transferred
Clarence Ryder here.

65
00:05:47,714 --> 00:05:49,649
Remember him?

66
00:05:49,816 --> 00:05:51,512
Yes, I remember him.

67
00:05:51,685 --> 00:05:55,918
I've done a lot of bad things in my
life, but I never ratted on anybody.

68
00:05:56,089 --> 00:05:57,853
It was a big mistake
testifying for you.

69
00:05:58,024 --> 00:05:59,544
I should never
have testified for you.

70
00:05:59,693 --> 00:06:02,390
You heard him confess.
He's a vicious killer.

71
00:06:02,562 --> 00:06:05,828
Your testimony
helped me convict him.

72
00:06:06,099 --> 00:06:10,867
Madam Prosecutor, I've
got some bad news for you.

73
00:06:12,105 --> 00:06:14,472
I lied.

74
00:06:25,185 --> 00:06:27,086
I'm sorry I'm late.

75
00:06:27,254 --> 00:06:28,745
I had to make some phone calls.

76
00:06:28,922 --> 00:06:30,322
- Is everything all right?
- Thanks.

77
00:06:30,490 --> 00:06:32,789
No, everything is lousy.

78
00:06:32,959 --> 00:06:36,327
Ben, I wanted tonight
to be so special,

79
00:06:36,496 --> 00:06:38,294
and now this has come up.

80
00:06:38,465 --> 00:06:41,697
- What?
- This inmate at the state prison,

81
00:06:41,868 --> 00:06:44,804
Earl McCutcheon,
called me at my hotel,

82
00:06:44,971 --> 00:06:48,874
said it was urgent, and so I
went out there this afternoon.

83
00:06:49,943 --> 00:06:52,879
Do you remember Clarence Ryder?

84
00:06:53,613 --> 00:06:57,380
I prosecuted him for the
murder of Brenda Chaney.

85
00:06:58,285 --> 00:06:59,878
Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.

86
00:07:00,053 --> 00:07:05,185
She was married to that
guy with Peachtree Cola,

87
00:07:05,358 --> 00:07:06,690
Phillip... Phillip Chaney.

88
00:07:06,860 --> 00:07:08,419
- Right.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

89
00:07:08,595 --> 00:07:12,259
Ryder was a
top-of-the-line thief.

90
00:07:13,266 --> 00:07:16,202
<i>Ryder was robbing
the Chaney house.</i>

91
00:07:16,369 --> 00:07:21,205
<i>He bypassed the alarm and
entered through a French door.</i>

92
00:07:23,310 --> 00:07:26,872
<i>He collected some
stuff from the living room,</i>

93
00:07:27,047 --> 00:07:29,209
<i>and then he went upstairs.</i>

94
00:07:31,551 --> 00:07:35,318
<i>Probably to get
Brenda Chaney's jewels.</i>

95
00:07:35,689 --> 00:07:39,421
<i>Well, she must have surprised him,
because he panicked and killed her.</i>

96
00:07:41,027 --> 00:07:45,089
<i>But before she died, she
managed to set off the alarm.</i>

97
00:07:45,865 --> 00:07:49,302
<i>Within an hour, Clarence
Ryder was arrested</i>

98
00:07:49,469 --> 00:07:52,598
<i>and the Chaney valuables
were found in his car.</i>

99
00:07:52,772 --> 00:07:57,540
Earl McCutcheon was
Clarence Ryder's cellmate.

100
00:07:58,211 --> 00:08:04,117
He testified that Clarence
Ryder told him in the jail

101
00:08:04,284 --> 00:08:07,311
that he had killed
Brenda Chaney.

102
00:08:07,487 --> 00:08:11,219
But today, he told me he lied.

103
00:08:11,391 --> 00:08:14,418
He said Clarence Ryder
never confessed to him,

104
00:08:14,594 --> 00:08:18,998
and that I sent an
innocent man to prison.

105
00:08:19,165 --> 00:08:22,397
Oh, Julie, I'm so sorry.

106
00:08:22,569 --> 00:08:24,800
Well, why'd he lie?

107
00:08:25,338 --> 00:08:27,364
He said

108
00:08:28,274 --> 00:08:32,644
the detective in charge
of the investigation

109
00:08:32,812 --> 00:08:34,337
told him what to say.

110
00:08:36,149 --> 00:08:39,313
Promised him an early
release if he perjured himself

111
00:08:39,486 --> 00:08:43,048
and a lot of extra
time if he didn't.

112
00:08:43,223 --> 00:08:44,953
So he lied.

113
00:08:45,825 --> 00:08:47,987
Earl McCutcheon.

114
00:08:48,395 --> 00:08:50,296
You think he's telling
the truth this time?

115
00:08:52,032 --> 00:08:54,263
I don't know what to think.

116
00:08:54,434 --> 00:08:55,834
Well, you know,

117
00:08:56,002 --> 00:09:00,133
maybe you better talk to that
detective he said put him up to it.

118
00:09:00,840 --> 00:09:02,672
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

119
00:09:09,015 --> 00:09:10,244
And you believed him?

120
00:09:10,417 --> 00:09:13,046
You shouldn't be talking to me.
You should be talking to a shrink.

121
00:09:13,219 --> 00:09:15,552
But you did help him
get an early release.

122
00:09:15,722 --> 00:09:17,953
Yeah, because he
helped me nail Ryder.

123
00:09:18,124 --> 00:09:19,683
Detective...

124
00:09:19,859 --> 00:09:23,125
Look, one hand washes the
other in this business, lady.

125
00:09:23,296 --> 00:09:25,697
That doesn't mean I bribed
the guy, for crying out loud.

126
00:09:25,865 --> 00:09:28,266
Do you always work weekends?

127
00:09:28,435 --> 00:09:31,633
Why? You asking me out?

128
00:09:32,605 --> 00:09:36,337
Earl McCutcheon says you came
to see him on a Saturday night.

129
00:09:36,509 --> 00:09:39,206
Well, now, if you're a cop, and
you're investigating a murder,

130
00:09:39,379 --> 00:09:41,109
and somebody calls you from jail

131
00:09:41,281 --> 00:09:43,807
and says that their cellmate
just confessed to that murder,

132
00:09:43,983 --> 00:09:45,474
wouldn't you go
down there right away?

133
00:09:45,652 --> 00:09:48,383
You're saying he called you?

134
00:09:48,555 --> 00:09:50,820
Of course he called me.

135
00:09:51,424 --> 00:09:53,689
Look, I'm a cop.

136
00:09:53,860 --> 00:09:55,920
Earl McCutcheon's a con, okay?

137
00:09:56,096 --> 00:09:59,225
Now, if you can't figure things
out from there, I can't help you.

138
00:09:59,399 --> 00:10:01,527
Nobody can.

139
00:10:23,923 --> 00:10:29,226
Just give me a second.
I'll be right there. Okay.

140
00:10:30,063 --> 00:10:32,999
Yeah, okay. Just a second now.

141
00:10:33,166 --> 00:10:35,067
Jiminy Crickets.

142
00:10:41,141 --> 00:10:42,268
- Ben? Oh.
- Yeah?

143
00:10:42,442 --> 00:10:44,877
- Hi. Can I come in?
- Hi.

144
00:10:45,345 --> 00:10:47,678
Thanks. I know I
should have called first,

145
00:10:47,847 --> 00:10:50,078
but I was so excited.

146
00:10:51,284 --> 00:10:54,846
Wow, the place looks great.

147
00:10:55,021 --> 00:10:57,513
New wallpaper.

148
00:10:57,757 --> 00:11:00,591
Um... Well,

149
00:11:01,294 --> 00:11:02,592
kind of.

150
00:11:02,762 --> 00:11:06,665
Okay, now, first
thing this morning,

151
00:11:06,833 --> 00:11:09,803
I talked to that detective,
Jake Van Owen,

152
00:11:09,969 --> 00:11:13,167
and he said Earl
McCutcheon called him

153
00:11:13,339 --> 00:11:15,365
from the jail on a Saturday

154
00:11:15,542 --> 00:11:17,704
and told him to get
down there right away

155
00:11:17,877 --> 00:11:20,403
because Clarence Ryder
had confessed to him.

156
00:11:20,580 --> 00:11:23,550
But Earl McCutcheon told me

157
00:11:23,716 --> 00:11:26,777
Detective Van Owen
initiated the contact

158
00:11:26,953 --> 00:11:29,821
by suddenly appearing
at the jail out of the blue.

159
00:11:29,989 --> 00:11:32,618
So I checked with
the telephone company

160
00:11:32,792 --> 00:11:35,159
to see who was
telling the truth.

161
00:11:35,328 --> 00:11:39,356
No calls were made
from the jail that Saturday,

162
00:11:39,532 --> 00:11:43,902
because on Friday
some inmate went berserk

163
00:11:44,070 --> 00:11:46,096
and ripped the
phone off the wall,

164
00:11:46,272 --> 00:11:48,332
and it didn't get
fixed until Monday.

165
00:11:48,508 --> 00:11:51,171
Detective Van
Owen lied to me, Ben.

166
00:11:51,344 --> 00:11:52,937
So I've gotta get
him out of there.

167
00:11:53,112 --> 00:11:56,173
I've got to get him a new
trial, but I'm gonna need help.

168
00:11:56,349 --> 00:11:58,318
No judge is gonna
let me defend him.

169
00:11:58,484 --> 00:12:00,680
I prosecuted him.

170
00:12:05,992 --> 00:12:07,893
Will you do it?

171
00:12:14,534 --> 00:12:18,801
- I'll try.
- Oh-ho.

172
00:12:20,440 --> 00:12:22,500
Whoopsie-doodle.

173
00:12:29,949 --> 00:12:32,976
We have reason to believe
you did not get a fair trial.

174
00:12:33,152 --> 00:12:35,314
She ought to know.
She railroaded me.

175
00:12:35,488 --> 00:12:38,014
She and that scum
bucket Earl McCutcheon.

176
00:12:38,191 --> 00:12:40,717
She got him to lie so
she could get a conviction.

177
00:12:40,893 --> 00:12:43,226
Somebody got him
to lie, but it wasn't her.

178
00:12:43,396 --> 00:12:45,695
Now, just settle down.

179
00:12:45,865 --> 00:12:48,334
I have to hear what
happened that night.

180
00:12:48,768 --> 00:12:50,430
Okay.

181
00:12:50,903 --> 00:12:53,134
For what it's worth.

182
00:12:53,873 --> 00:12:57,002
I hit the place thinking
Phillip Chaney was still at work

183
00:12:57,176 --> 00:13:00,408
and that his wife was off playing
tennis somewhere like usual.

184
00:13:00,580 --> 00:13:03,914
- You'd been watching them?
- I had one rule:

185
00:13:04,083 --> 00:13:07,918
Never hit a house when
somebody was home.

186
00:13:08,588 --> 00:13:11,854
<i>It was around 6 or 6:30. I
let myself in the back door.</i>

187
00:13:13,359 --> 00:13:17,262
<i>I grabbed some stuff in the living
room when I heard some noise.</i>

188
00:13:17,430 --> 00:13:20,525
<i>I'd been coming there
every night for three weeks.</i>

189
00:13:20,700 --> 00:13:22,464
<i>Nobody home on Wednesday night.</i>

190
00:13:22,635 --> 00:13:24,126
<i>Not even a car in sight.</i>

191
00:13:25,538 --> 00:13:28,736
<i>When I heard somebody
upstairs, I got the hell out of there.</i>

192
00:13:28,908 --> 00:13:30,467
You never went up the stairs?

193
00:13:30,643 --> 00:13:34,910
I never even saw the
woman, much less killed her.

194
00:13:35,782 --> 00:13:37,341
So, what's the deal?
Why is everybody

195
00:13:37,517 --> 00:13:39,952
so interested in the
truth all of a sudden?

196
00:13:40,119 --> 00:13:43,146
Earl McCutcheon
has admitted he lied.

197
00:13:43,323 --> 00:13:46,919
So if you want a new trial,
we can probably get you one.

198
00:13:47,093 --> 00:13:49,562
Well, come on. What do you say?

199
00:13:57,303 --> 00:13:58,601
You're gonna reopen the case?

200
00:13:58,771 --> 00:14:02,003
I'm sorry, but it
has to be done.

201
00:14:02,608 --> 00:14:04,201
Tell C.J. and Bill to come in.

202
00:14:04,377 --> 00:14:06,710
Call Parker, tell him to
start the presentation.

203
00:14:06,879 --> 00:14:09,610
- I'll be up in about five minutes.
- Yes, sir.

204
00:14:10,350 --> 00:14:13,514
I remember how painful
the trial was for you, Phillip.

205
00:14:14,487 --> 00:14:16,251
I thought you should
hear this from me

206
00:14:16,422 --> 00:14:18,516
before you read
it in the papers.

207
00:14:19,158 --> 00:14:21,423
I was just beginning to
put all this behind me.

208
00:14:21,594 --> 00:14:24,291
I know. But there's
an old saying:

209
00:14:24,464 --> 00:14:29,027
"Better a hundred guilty men go
free than one innocent man go to jail."

210
00:14:30,536 --> 00:14:32,232
I guess.

211
00:14:34,440 --> 00:14:37,672
- Any leads on the real killer?
- No.

212
00:14:38,745 --> 00:14:40,543
How can a man get
up on the stand and lie?

213
00:14:40,713 --> 00:14:41,772
I don't understand that.

214
00:14:41,948 --> 00:14:44,144
Neither do I.

215
00:14:44,317 --> 00:14:45,751
We're here.

216
00:14:46,018 --> 00:14:49,284
The idea of listening to all
that stuff about Brenda's death,

217
00:14:49,455 --> 00:14:51,424
I don't know if I can go
through all that again.

218
00:14:51,591 --> 00:14:53,082
I understand.

219
00:14:53,259 --> 00:14:56,127
May...? May I use your phone?

220
00:14:56,295 --> 00:14:59,288
- Help yourself. Dial nine.
- Thanks.

221
00:14:59,465 --> 00:15:00,524
- Fine.
- Yes.

222
00:15:17,083 --> 00:15:19,484
Jake? Think it was
Jake Van Owen?

223
00:15:19,652 --> 00:15:20,847
I know it was.

224
00:15:21,020 --> 00:15:23,285
I recognized the number.

225
00:15:23,456 --> 00:15:26,085
Now, why would he be
calling Phillip Chaney?

226
00:15:26,259 --> 00:15:29,388
Well, probably to tell
him what you told him.

227
00:15:30,463 --> 00:15:32,591
But the only thing
Detective Van Owen knows

228
00:15:32,765 --> 00:15:36,065
is that Earl McCutcheon
recanted his story.

229
00:15:37,103 --> 00:15:39,732
He doesn't know we're
moving for a new trial.

230
00:15:40,339 --> 00:15:44,208
The only person I've
told is Phillip Chaney.

231
00:15:44,377 --> 00:15:48,246
Well, maybe they got to know
each other during the first trial.

232
00:15:48,414 --> 00:15:51,350
I don't think they said
two words to each other.

233
00:15:51,517 --> 00:15:53,349
And I was there
every day, remember?

234
00:15:53,519 --> 00:15:56,353
Well, you know, I could take
a look at the phone records

235
00:15:56,522 --> 00:15:58,650
just to see how much contact
they had with each other

236
00:15:58,825 --> 00:16:00,054
during the time of the trial.

237
00:16:00,226 --> 00:16:01,785
- Would you?
- Sure.

238
00:16:02,295 --> 00:16:04,958
There you are. A little
mustard, a lot of relish.

239
00:16:05,498 --> 00:16:07,865
Oh, Ben, you remembered.

240
00:16:11,003 --> 00:16:13,598
Here. Put something
in your mouth.

241
00:16:14,640 --> 00:16:17,007
So, Julie, I guess this means
you're gonna be spending

242
00:16:17,176 --> 00:16:19,907
a lot more time in Atlanta.
More than you figured, huh?

243
00:16:20,079 --> 00:16:21,240
I guess.

244
00:16:21,414 --> 00:16:24,578
Well, you're welcome to
crash at my place if you'd like.

245
00:16:24,750 --> 00:16:26,912
Oh, the hotel's fine.

246
00:16:27,086 --> 00:16:29,112
- But thanks anyway.
- Sure.

247
00:16:33,159 --> 00:16:34,821
There you go.

248
00:16:35,761 --> 00:16:38,196
This has ketchup all over it.

249
00:16:38,364 --> 00:16:40,526
I don't like ketchup.

250
00:16:41,701 --> 00:16:43,260
I know.

251
00:16:49,408 --> 00:16:51,377
- She's over there.
- Thank you.

252
00:16:59,685 --> 00:17:01,210
Excuse me, Mrs. Kellogg?

253
00:17:01,387 --> 00:17:03,788
Hi. I'm Jerri Stone. I'm
a private investigator.

254
00:17:03,956 --> 00:17:06,076
I was wondering if I could
ask you a couple questions.

255
00:17:06,225 --> 00:17:07,284
Private investigator?

256
00:17:07,460 --> 00:17:10,487
Yeah, I work for a lawyer
here in town, Ben Matlock.

257
00:17:10,663 --> 00:17:13,223
- Have I done something?
- No. No, no. It's nothing like that.

258
00:17:13,399 --> 00:17:18,133
In fact, keep watering. I'd hate to
see these flowers die on my account.

259
00:17:20,806 --> 00:17:22,638
Oh, are these petunias?

260
00:17:22,808 --> 00:17:24,606
No, African violets.

261
00:17:25,111 --> 00:17:26,443
Oh.

262
00:17:26,612 --> 00:17:28,205
Well, it shows you
how much I know.

263
00:17:28,381 --> 00:17:31,215
I don't do well with plants.
I buy them, I kill them.

264
00:17:31,384 --> 00:17:33,751
It's like a law of nature.

265
00:17:33,920 --> 00:17:36,287
I've never talked to a
private investigator before.

266
00:17:36,455 --> 00:17:38,321
What do you wanna know?

267
00:17:38,724 --> 00:17:42,354
I'm looking into a murder
that occurred some time ago.

268
00:17:42,528 --> 00:17:44,520
The victim's name
was Brenda Chaney.

269
00:17:44,697 --> 00:17:46,188
Was she a friend of yours?

270
00:17:46,365 --> 00:17:48,197
Chaney? No.

271
00:17:48,367 --> 00:17:50,529
I don't think I know
anyone named Chaney.

272
00:17:51,804 --> 00:17:54,501
Well, I happened to be looking
over some old phone records,

273
00:17:54,674 --> 00:17:58,167
and I noticed that on
October 5th, three years ago,

274
00:17:58,344 --> 00:18:00,609
somebody from her
residence called your residence

275
00:18:00,780 --> 00:18:06,879
at 1:57 in the afternoon,
and talked for 83 minutes.

276
00:18:07,053 --> 00:18:08,885
October 5th?

277
00:18:09,055 --> 00:18:10,114
Yeah.

278
00:18:10,289 --> 00:18:12,952
Eighty-three minutes seems
like a long time to talk somebody,

279
00:18:13,125 --> 00:18:17,620
- especially if you don't know them.
- That whole week is a blur.

280
00:18:17,797 --> 00:18:22,735
My daughter was killed in
a car accident on the 3rd.

281
00:18:22,902 --> 00:18:24,461
Hit and run.

282
00:18:24,637 --> 00:18:25,935
She was 10.

283
00:18:26,105 --> 00:18:27,630
Oh, I'm sorry.

284
00:18:27,807 --> 00:18:31,335
So the phone rang
constantly for days.

285
00:18:33,713 --> 00:18:37,206
But, you know, come to think of
it, I did have a lengthy conversation

286
00:18:37,383 --> 00:18:39,375
with some woman I
didn't know around then.

287
00:18:39,552 --> 00:18:41,077
She called to say
she'd been so moved

288
00:18:41,253 --> 00:18:44,655
by what she'd read in the
newspaper about Cammie's...

289
00:18:44,824 --> 00:18:47,817
That she wanted to start
a fund in Cammie's name

290
00:18:47,994 --> 00:18:49,656
for needy children or something.

291
00:18:49,829 --> 00:18:53,994
But I don't remember what
she said her name was.

292
00:18:54,166 --> 00:18:57,261
She was nice, compassionate.

293
00:18:57,436 --> 00:19:01,032
She never started a fund, of
course, but it was a nice thought.

294
00:19:03,709 --> 00:19:06,304
Could be she never
got a chance to.

295
00:19:07,046 --> 00:19:09,015
If it was Brenda Chaney,

296
00:19:09,181 --> 00:19:11,776
she was murdered
less than five hours later.

297
00:19:17,256 --> 00:19:20,158
What has occurred
is the worst nightmare

298
00:19:20,326 --> 00:19:24,195
of everyone involved with
the American system of justice.

299
00:19:24,363 --> 00:19:28,801
A man has been incarcerated
for a crime he did not commit.

300
00:19:29,168 --> 00:19:33,230
The deposition I just gave
you is by Earl McCutcheon,

301
00:19:33,406 --> 00:19:37,707
the man to whom Clarence
Ryder allegedly confessed.

302
00:19:37,877 --> 00:19:43,009
The man who relayed that confession
to the court during Mr. Ryder's trial.

303
00:19:43,182 --> 00:19:48,177
The man who, as you will
see, now admits he lied.

304
00:19:48,354 --> 00:19:51,756
And because he lied,
Clarence Ryder went to prison.

305
00:19:51,924 --> 00:19:56,385
It's a wrong that must be
righted, Your Honor, and soon.

306
00:19:56,562 --> 00:20:00,590
Because to be unjustly
punished in the name of justice

307
00:20:00,766 --> 00:20:03,702
is the worst injustice of all.

308
00:20:03,869 --> 00:20:05,269
Thank you.

309
00:20:07,239 --> 00:20:08,764
Does the State
have any comments?

310
00:20:08,941 --> 00:20:10,341
Yes, indeed, Your Honor.

311
00:20:11,610 --> 00:20:14,410
The defense would like you to believe
that Clarence Ryder was convicted

312
00:20:14,580 --> 00:20:17,140
based on the testimony
of just one man.

313
00:20:17,316 --> 00:20:18,909
That's just not true.

314
00:20:19,085 --> 00:20:21,281
Forensic evidence showed
that he was in the Chaney home

315
00:20:21,454 --> 00:20:22,854
the night of the murder.

316
00:20:23,022 --> 00:20:24,547
And items stolen from that home

317
00:20:24,724 --> 00:20:26,693
- were found in his
possession - You were great.

318
00:20:26,859 --> 00:20:28,179
After the burglar
alarm went off.

319
00:20:28,327 --> 00:20:30,558
How does it feel sitting
on this side of the aisle?

320
00:20:30,730 --> 00:20:34,292
Strange. But the
company's better.

321
00:20:41,373 --> 00:20:42,864
- How'd it go?
- Pretty good, I think.

322
00:20:43,042 --> 00:20:46,171
The judge retired to
chambers to make his decision.

323
00:20:46,345 --> 00:20:48,610
Well, I think I may have
something, but I'm not sure.

324
00:20:48,781 --> 00:20:50,181
Come on.

325
00:20:52,251 --> 00:20:53,378
Let's hear it.

326
00:20:53,552 --> 00:20:55,282
Well, on the day of the murder,

327
00:20:55,454 --> 00:20:58,390
somebody from the Chaney
house, probably Brenda Chaney,

328
00:20:58,557 --> 00:21:00,583
called a woman
named Susan Kellogg

329
00:21:00,760 --> 00:21:03,320
and talked to her for
an hour and 20 minutes.

330
00:21:03,496 --> 00:21:04,896
Who's Susan Kellogg?

331
00:21:05,698 --> 00:21:07,018
She's the mother
of a little girl

332
00:21:07,166 --> 00:21:09,897
who was killed by a hit-and-run
driver two days before.

333
00:21:10,069 --> 00:21:12,334
So I looked up
the accident report,

334
00:21:12,505 --> 00:21:15,839
and guess what homicide
detective was assigned to the case?

335
00:21:16,008 --> 00:21:17,567
Jake Van Owen?

336
00:21:19,345 --> 00:21:22,372
Here's his signature
on the initial report.

337
00:21:22,548 --> 00:21:24,244
That's all I could get
without a subpoena.

338
00:21:24,717 --> 00:21:25,776
Let's get you a subpoena.

339
00:21:27,787 --> 00:21:31,019
After studying the briefs and
depositions presented to me,

340
00:21:31,190 --> 00:21:32,870
and listening to
arguments from both sides,

341
00:21:34,326 --> 00:21:37,194
I have concluded that
there is indeed just cause

342
00:21:37,363 --> 00:21:39,594
to set aside the
original verdict

343
00:21:39,765 --> 00:21:41,461
and to hold a new trial,

344
00:21:41,634 --> 00:21:45,002
the date of which is to
be set as soon as possible.

345
00:21:51,377 --> 00:21:55,144
- Ah. We did it.
- Yeah.

346
00:21:55,514 --> 00:21:57,608
- We did it.
- Yeah.

347
00:21:57,783 --> 00:22:01,720
Well, we're still a long ways
from the barn, but it's a start.

348
00:22:01,887 --> 00:22:03,981
- Here's to you, Ben.
- Oh, to you.

349
00:22:04,156 --> 00:22:06,557
- You're really special.
- So are you.

350
00:22:14,266 --> 00:22:18,601
Well, now, finally, we can talk
about something other than the case.

351
00:22:18,771 --> 00:22:20,296
Yeah.

352
00:22:21,207 --> 00:22:24,507
That seminar was just an excuse.

353
00:22:24,944 --> 00:22:28,642
I mostly came to
Atlanta to see you.

354
00:22:29,849 --> 00:22:31,215
Oh.

355
00:22:31,483 --> 00:22:34,453
There are just some things
you can't say over the phone.

356
00:22:34,620 --> 00:22:35,952
- Yeah.
- I mean, you have

357
00:22:36,121 --> 00:22:39,114
- to say them face to face.
- That's right.

358
00:22:39,291 --> 00:22:41,385
- I know.
- So...

359
00:22:41,760 --> 00:22:45,492
I hope this makes you
as happy as it makes me.

360
00:22:49,768 --> 00:22:52,363
I'm getting married, Ben.

361
00:23:07,620 --> 00:23:09,782
Um... You're
getting married? Oh.

362
00:23:09,955 --> 00:23:12,550
Oh, well, how about that?

363
00:23:12,725 --> 00:23:14,193
Congratulations.

364
00:23:14,360 --> 00:23:15,851
Thanks.

365
00:23:19,164 --> 00:23:22,225
So, what's he do?

366
00:23:22,401 --> 00:23:24,632
He's an eye surgeon.

367
00:23:25,170 --> 00:23:30,734
His name is Michael Jensen,
and he's tops in his field.

368
00:23:31,677 --> 00:23:36,741
Oh, well, a lawyer and a doctor
has a nice ring to it, doesn't it?

369
00:23:36,916 --> 00:23:40,683
Yes, well, he's quite
a guy, that's for sure.

370
00:23:40,853 --> 00:23:42,253
Yeah.

371
00:23:46,425 --> 00:23:50,886
Well, getting married.
How about that?

372
00:23:52,598 --> 00:23:54,328
Second time's the charm, right?

373
00:23:56,702 --> 00:23:58,193
Yeah.

374
00:24:00,072 --> 00:24:04,032
Did you ever, ahem, think
of getting married again?

375
00:24:05,945 --> 00:24:07,573
No.

376
00:24:08,881 --> 00:24:11,407
Well, sometimes I...

377
00:24:12,718 --> 00:24:14,516
I don't know.

378
00:24:22,995 --> 00:24:24,759
So...

379
00:24:26,932 --> 00:24:29,026
What do you think?

380
00:24:30,636 --> 00:24:34,596
Oh, well, it's,
uh... Uh... Uh...

381
00:24:35,541 --> 00:24:37,203
Wanna order?

382
00:25:10,042 --> 00:25:11,670
You, uh...?

383
00:25:12,111 --> 00:25:14,171
You Jerri Stone?

384
00:25:14,346 --> 00:25:15,405
Yeah.

385
00:25:16,181 --> 00:25:17,740
You're a girl.

386
00:25:19,818 --> 00:25:21,013
And you're a boy.

387
00:25:21,653 --> 00:25:23,315
Whose name is...?

388
00:25:23,489 --> 00:25:24,787
Jake Van Owen.

389
00:25:24,957 --> 00:25:27,552
Jake Van Owen. Well,
what a coincidence.

390
00:25:27,726 --> 00:25:30,046
Because I just happened to
be going over your investigation

391
00:25:30,195 --> 00:25:33,256
of the hit-and-run case
that killed Camille Kellogg.

392
00:25:34,299 --> 00:25:36,268
Yeah, so I heard.

393
00:25:37,036 --> 00:25:40,302
As long as you're here, maybe you
wouldn't mind answering questions.

394
00:25:42,374 --> 00:25:43,535
Like what?

395
00:25:44,877 --> 00:25:49,645
Oh, like, how come you
never found the driver?

396
00:25:52,651 --> 00:25:54,119
No witnesses.

397
00:25:54,286 --> 00:25:55,879
We had nothing to go on.

398
00:25:57,256 --> 00:25:59,885
Well, you had all the physical
evidence from the scene.

399
00:26:01,093 --> 00:26:04,655
A couple of pieces of
glass took us nowhere.

400
00:26:06,165 --> 00:26:07,565
Look, lady.

401
00:26:07,733 --> 00:26:09,793
Don't think I don't
know what you're up to.

402
00:26:10,636 --> 00:26:12,730
Don't you like what I'm up to?

403
00:26:12,905 --> 00:26:15,374
You're damn right I don't.

404
00:26:16,075 --> 00:26:18,374
You're trying to find something
that'll make me look bad,

405
00:26:18,544 --> 00:26:21,708
so that Earl McCutcheon will look
good when your boss puts the lying dirtball

406
00:26:21,880 --> 00:26:23,781
on the stand.

407
00:26:24,383 --> 00:26:27,842
You turned him
into a lying dirtball.

408
00:26:28,020 --> 00:26:30,717
I'm just trying
to figure out why.

409
00:26:31,423 --> 00:26:35,190
He was telling the truth when
he testified the first time, lady.

410
00:26:35,360 --> 00:26:37,761
He's lying now.

411
00:26:41,767 --> 00:26:44,236
Don't say I didn't warn you.

412
00:26:52,144 --> 00:26:54,636
Want some coffee? Just made.

413
00:26:54,813 --> 00:26:56,247
- That'd be great.
- Okay.

414
00:26:56,415 --> 00:26:59,715
- You heard from Jerri?
- Not yet. No, not yet.

415
00:27:00,419 --> 00:27:01,443
There you are.

416
00:27:01,620 --> 00:27:02,644
- Thanks.
- Yeah.

417
00:27:02,821 --> 00:27:04,221
- Ben?
- Hmm?

418
00:27:04,656 --> 00:27:07,922
Last night, when we
were having dinner, I...

419
00:27:08,093 --> 00:27:11,359
Um... This is really awkward.

420
00:27:11,530 --> 00:27:14,830
I don't know how to say
this without being direct.

421
00:27:15,000 --> 00:27:16,491
Say what?

422
00:27:16,668 --> 00:27:22,835
Last night, I was trying to get you
to tell me how you feel about me.

423
00:27:23,842 --> 00:27:24,866
Oh!

424
00:27:26,645 --> 00:27:28,876
You know how I feel about you.

425
00:27:29,047 --> 00:27:31,710
- No, I don't.
- Well, sure you do.

426
00:27:31,884 --> 00:27:35,343
No. No. Ben, I don't.

427
00:27:35,521 --> 00:27:40,892
Our relationship is something
we have never, ever, discussed.

428
00:27:41,193 --> 00:27:45,289
I don't know where
I stand with you.

429
00:27:46,598 --> 00:27:49,329
Cream. Cream. You take cream.

430
00:27:49,501 --> 00:27:52,699
I don't need cream.
I need an answer.

431
00:27:52,871 --> 00:27:55,306
- Cream, cream, cream.
- Ben?

432
00:27:55,474 --> 00:27:57,466
Ben, this is important to me.

433
00:27:57,643 --> 00:27:59,942
I'm about to marry Michael.

434
00:28:00,112 --> 00:28:05,813
Now, I know you're not very
good at expressing your feelings,

435
00:28:05,984 --> 00:28:09,944
but, please, just this once,

436
00:28:10,889 --> 00:28:12,448
try.

437
00:28:35,447 --> 00:28:37,973
- Ben?
- In the kitchen.

438
00:28:40,118 --> 00:28:42,383
- Hi.
- Hi.

439
00:28:42,554 --> 00:28:44,887
Oh. I'm sorry. Am I
interrupting something?

440
00:28:45,057 --> 00:28:48,118
Oh, no. No, no. Here,
have some coffee. Sit down.

441
00:28:48,293 --> 00:28:49,420
- Okay.
- Find anything?

442
00:28:49,595 --> 00:28:51,461
No, not really.

443
00:28:51,630 --> 00:28:54,429
Nobody actually
saw the accident.

444
00:28:54,600 --> 00:28:57,434
And the two people that said
they saw the car speeding away

445
00:28:57,603 --> 00:28:59,469
couldn't agree on
what color the car was,

446
00:28:59,638 --> 00:29:01,402
let alone what the
driver looked like.

447
00:29:01,573 --> 00:29:03,405
The case was never solved?

448
00:29:03,575 --> 00:29:07,137
No. All they had to go on was
a piece of broken headlight.

449
00:29:07,312 --> 00:29:10,077
They were able to determine
that it came from a Mercedes,

450
00:29:10,249 --> 00:29:12,809
so they checked with all
the dealerships in the area

451
00:29:12,985 --> 00:29:15,386
to see if they'd replaced
any broken headlights

452
00:29:15,554 --> 00:29:18,023
or done any suspicious bodywork.

453
00:29:18,190 --> 00:29:19,715
Nothing.

454
00:29:19,891 --> 00:29:21,621
Van Owen got nowhere.

455
00:29:21,793 --> 00:29:24,024
And to tell you the
truth, I'm not surprised.

456
00:29:24,196 --> 00:29:27,223
Now, here's a
coincidence for you.

457
00:29:27,866 --> 00:29:31,394
Phillip Chaney
drives a Mercedes.

458
00:29:32,471 --> 00:29:35,464
I remember it from the trial.

459
00:29:42,247 --> 00:29:43,613
Julie, am I being sensitive

460
00:29:43,782 --> 00:29:47,241
or are you suggesting that
Brenda and I hit that little girl?

461
00:29:48,487 --> 00:29:51,889
Well, it's a possibility
that's come up.

462
00:29:55,527 --> 00:29:57,428
Well, let's see.

463
00:29:58,764 --> 00:30:02,462
October 3rd.

464
00:30:07,439 --> 00:30:10,534
We had dinner with the
Davenports at their house at 7.

465
00:30:10,709 --> 00:30:13,736
Do you happen to remember
what time you got home?

466
00:30:14,413 --> 00:30:17,315
- No.
- Where did the Davenports live?

467
00:30:17,482 --> 00:30:19,280
Forest Ridge.

468
00:30:19,451 --> 00:30:21,818
That's not far from
where the Kelloggs live.

469
00:30:22,487 --> 00:30:23,716
You are out of line.

470
00:30:23,889 --> 00:30:26,324
Oh, one more thing.
I checked my files,

471
00:30:26,491 --> 00:30:29,256
and on the night
Brenda was murdered,

472
00:30:29,428 --> 00:30:33,160
you went to your friend Arnold
Kruck's 50th birthday party.

473
00:30:34,566 --> 00:30:36,762
- That's right.
- Brenda was supposed to go,

474
00:30:36,935 --> 00:30:39,564
but she didn't feel well
so she stayed home.

475
00:30:41,640 --> 00:30:43,632
Do you remember what
time you got to the party

476
00:30:43,809 --> 00:30:45,835
and what time you left?

477
00:30:56,321 --> 00:30:58,881
The party was
at 6. I left about 8.

478
00:30:59,057 --> 00:31:01,356
I got home to find my
house swarming with police

479
00:31:01,526 --> 00:31:04,086
who told me my wife was dead.

480
00:31:05,697 --> 00:31:07,461
Any other questions?

481
00:31:07,632 --> 00:31:10,261
I'm just doing my job, Phillip.

482
00:31:11,703 --> 00:31:15,231
Then why don't you
get out so I can do mine?

483
00:31:26,551 --> 00:31:28,315
I talked to Mary Kruck,

484
00:31:28,487 --> 00:31:31,389
and she said Phillip Chaney
was definitely at the party,

485
00:31:31,556 --> 00:31:34,476
but she couldn't say for sure what
time he got there or what time he left.

486
00:31:34,626 --> 00:31:36,219
- But she had that picture.
- Hmm.

487
00:31:36,395 --> 00:31:41,026
Which I got blown up to
see what time his watch said.

488
00:31:42,467 --> 00:31:43,799
Uh...

489
00:31:44,436 --> 00:31:46,701
Six-twenty.

490
00:31:46,872 --> 00:31:49,706
The burglar alarm
went off at 6:36,

491
00:31:49,875 --> 00:31:52,811
and the police got
there five minutes later.

492
00:31:52,978 --> 00:31:54,776
No way that Phillip
Chaney could've done it.

493
00:31:54,946 --> 00:31:57,313
Well, worth a try.

494
00:32:01,420 --> 00:32:03,753
What did the
defendant say to you

495
00:32:03,922 --> 00:32:06,824
when you shared
his cell that weekend?

496
00:32:07,559 --> 00:32:09,118
Nothing, really.

497
00:32:09,294 --> 00:32:11,229
He wasn't much of a talker.

498
00:32:11,396 --> 00:32:14,992
He didn't confess to you that he
had murdered Brenda Chaney?

499
00:32:15,667 --> 00:32:17,499
No, he did not.

500
00:32:18,003 --> 00:32:20,996
Well, why did you perjure
yourself at the first trial

501
00:32:21,173 --> 00:32:23,233
by saying he had confessed?

502
00:32:23,408 --> 00:32:25,741
Because a certain member
of the police force told me

503
00:32:25,911 --> 00:32:28,176
that I'd be released
early if I did,

504
00:32:28,780 --> 00:32:30,908
and real late if I didn't.

505
00:32:32,217 --> 00:32:34,118
No further questions.

506
00:32:34,553 --> 00:32:35,953
Cross examine?

507
00:32:36,121 --> 00:32:37,612
Please.

508
00:32:41,393 --> 00:32:43,385
Let me get this straight.

509
00:32:43,795 --> 00:32:45,229
You stood up there
three years ago,

510
00:32:45,397 --> 00:32:47,457
took the oath and then
lied. Is that correct?

511
00:32:49,301 --> 00:32:50,564
I'm afraid so.

512
00:32:50,735 --> 00:32:53,136
But this time you took the
oath and you're telling the truth?

513
00:32:53,772 --> 00:32:55,400
Right.

514
00:32:55,574 --> 00:32:58,009
Well, no offense, Mr. McCutcheon,
but how do we know that?

515
00:32:58,176 --> 00:32:59,576
How do we know
you're not lying now?

516
00:33:00,412 --> 00:33:03,644
Well, because I'm not.

517
00:33:03,815 --> 00:33:06,307
Where did you say
you currently reside?

518
00:33:06,485 --> 00:33:08,113
Atlanta State Prison.

519
00:33:08,286 --> 00:33:10,152
Anybody else in this
room reside there?

520
00:33:12,290 --> 00:33:13,690
He does.

521
00:33:13,859 --> 00:33:17,591
Let the record show he pointed
to the defendant, Clarence Ryder.

522
00:33:17,762 --> 00:33:19,594
Has Mr. Ryder been
threatening you, Earl?

523
00:33:21,867 --> 00:33:23,802
Has he been saying
he's gonna get back at you

524
00:33:23,969 --> 00:33:26,404
for testifying against
him during the first trial?

525
00:33:29,541 --> 00:33:32,238
Well, not to my face.

526
00:33:32,410 --> 00:33:35,403
No. But word gets
around in prison, doesn't it?

527
00:33:35,580 --> 00:33:39,073
He's been making threats you're
afraid he's gonna make good on.

528
00:33:39,251 --> 00:33:41,686
And since you're both lifers,
you figured the best way

529
00:33:41,853 --> 00:33:44,982
to stay alive and healthy
was to placate him

530
00:33:45,156 --> 00:33:47,716
by recanting your
story, didn't you?

531
00:33:48,460 --> 00:33:50,258
Didn't you?

532
00:33:52,230 --> 00:33:54,199
The guards said they
couldn't protect me.

533
00:33:54,366 --> 00:33:56,164
Now, wait just a minute!

534
00:33:56,334 --> 00:33:57,962
Miss March, you're out of order.

535
00:33:58,136 --> 00:33:59,866
Julie.

536
00:34:00,038 --> 00:34:02,098
No use getting all
mad and out of sorts.

537
00:34:02,274 --> 00:34:03,537
It won't get you anywhere.

538
00:34:03,708 --> 00:34:06,068
So three years ago, you were
telling the truth, weren't you?

539
00:34:06,211 --> 00:34:08,339
Clarence Ryder did
confess to you in that jail cell.

540
00:34:08,513 --> 00:34:10,948
- Look, I...
- The truth, Mr. McCutcheon!

541
00:34:11,116 --> 00:34:13,312
He told you he murdered
Brenda Chaney, didn't he?

542
00:34:20,025 --> 00:34:22,893
- Yes.
- Now, wait a minute!

543
00:34:23,061 --> 00:34:26,554
Mr. Matlock, I will tolerate
no outbursts from you either.

544
00:34:26,731 --> 00:34:28,063
Somebody got to
you, didn't they?

545
00:34:28,233 --> 00:34:30,634
- Mr. Matlock, sit down.
- Who...? Who was it?

546
00:34:30,802 --> 00:34:32,202
I said, sit down!

547
00:34:32,370 --> 00:34:34,032
But, Your Honor,
somebody got to him!

548
00:34:34,205 --> 00:34:35,867
Every time something happens

549
00:34:36,041 --> 00:34:38,476
in this courtroom
that you disagree with,

550
00:34:38,643 --> 00:34:41,738
you start yelling and
flailing your arms about,

551
00:34:41,913 --> 00:34:43,211
and I'm sick of it!

552
00:34:43,381 --> 00:34:45,179
Now, sit down!

553
00:34:46,017 --> 00:34:47,485
Ben.

554
00:35:01,366 --> 00:35:03,995
Earl McCutcheon
still won't talk to me.

555
00:35:04,169 --> 00:35:07,867
I can't believe he
sandbagged us like that.

556
00:35:08,039 --> 00:35:10,941
That detective
probably got to him.

557
00:35:11,977 --> 00:35:15,539
Maybe we should subpoena
Detective Van Owen.

558
00:35:15,847 --> 00:35:19,614
He'd just lie and we wouldn't
be able to prove anything.

559
00:35:19,851 --> 00:35:21,251
What are we gonna do?

560
00:35:21,419 --> 00:35:23,650
Well, Cliff and Jerri are
checking bank records.

561
00:35:23,822 --> 00:35:25,120
Maybe there's some kind of link

562
00:35:25,290 --> 00:35:27,486
between this Phillip
Chaney and that detective.

563
00:35:27,659 --> 00:35:31,562
No, they're too smart
to leave a money trail.

564
00:35:33,198 --> 00:35:36,430
Mike thinks I should just forget
the whole thing and come home.

565
00:35:37,402 --> 00:35:38,870
Mike?

566
00:35:39,871 --> 00:35:42,841
Oh, yeah, your eye guy.

567
00:35:43,008 --> 00:35:47,309
He says even if we did manage
to get Clarence Ryder out of prison,

568
00:35:47,479 --> 00:35:50,540
he'd probably just commit some
other crime and be sent back.

569
00:35:51,049 --> 00:35:56,078
He might realize his mistakes
and lead a better life. You can't tell.

570
00:35:58,990 --> 00:36:00,549
- Ben?
- Hmm?

571
00:36:02,894 --> 00:36:04,920
Ben, I have to know.

572
00:36:05,096 --> 00:36:06,496
What?

573
00:36:06,665 --> 00:36:08,827
You and me. Are you listening?

574
00:36:09,000 --> 00:36:11,333
- Look at this.
- No, no, no.

575
00:36:11,503 --> 00:36:14,530
This time you are not
going to change the subject.

576
00:36:14,706 --> 00:36:17,175
No, look... Look
in the mirror here.

577
00:36:23,715 --> 00:36:25,843
- They served birthday cake.
- Yeah.

578
00:36:26,718 --> 00:36:28,550
Twenty minutes into the party?

579
00:36:36,261 --> 00:36:38,958
Yes, Detective Van Owen
was the officer in charge

580
00:36:39,130 --> 00:36:41,395
of the investigation.

581
00:36:41,566 --> 00:36:43,159
As I recall, he was
the one that met me

582
00:36:43,334 --> 00:36:47,601
at the door to my house that
night and told me my wife was dead.

583
00:36:48,440 --> 00:36:50,807
Are you sure you didn't
meet him before that,

584
00:36:50,975 --> 00:36:54,036
while he was
investigating a hit-and-run

585
00:36:54,212 --> 00:36:56,477
in which a 10-year-old
girl was killed?

586
00:36:56,648 --> 00:36:57,672
Positive.

587
00:36:57,849 --> 00:36:59,249
He never questioned you

588
00:36:59,417 --> 00:37:00,783
- about that accident?
- No.

589
00:37:00,952 --> 00:37:03,820
Now, now. Now, wait now. Uh...

590
00:37:05,356 --> 00:37:09,657
According to these
telephone records, someone...

591
00:37:09,828 --> 00:37:11,057
Your wife.

592
00:37:11,229 --> 00:37:13,391
Called a Susan Kellogg.

593
00:37:13,565 --> 00:37:17,127
The mother of the girl who was
killed, a woman she had never met,

594
00:37:17,302 --> 00:37:20,704
and talked to her for over an
hour two days after the accident.

595
00:37:20,872 --> 00:37:22,135
Now, why would she do that?

596
00:37:22,307 --> 00:37:23,866
How can you be so
sure it was my wife?

597
00:37:24,042 --> 00:37:25,635
There's people in
my house all the time.

598
00:37:25,810 --> 00:37:27,676
People in your
house? What people?

599
00:37:27,846 --> 00:37:29,439
People working
there? People visiting?

600
00:37:29,614 --> 00:37:32,106
Why would these
people call Susan Kellogg,

601
00:37:32,283 --> 00:37:34,616
a person they probably
never heard of?

602
00:37:36,054 --> 00:37:37,818
How should I know?

603
00:37:38,490 --> 00:37:39,514
When, uh, ahem...

604
00:37:39,691 --> 00:37:45,062
When we first spoke
to Susan about that call,

605
00:37:45,730 --> 00:37:47,722
because of the pain
of losing her daughter

606
00:37:47,899 --> 00:37:50,892
in such a tragic way,
she couldn't remember.

607
00:37:51,069 --> 00:37:54,335
But over time, she
does remember.

608
00:37:54,506 --> 00:38:00,969
The voice on the other end of
the line said the name Chaney.

609
00:38:04,883 --> 00:38:07,148
Are you sure your
wife didn't make that call

610
00:38:07,318 --> 00:38:11,722
because either you or she or both
were responsible for that hit-and-run

611
00:38:11,890 --> 00:38:12,949
and she felt guilty?

612
00:38:13,124 --> 00:38:14,490
No. Absolutely not.

613
00:38:14,659 --> 00:38:17,754
Isn't it true that when
Detective Van Owen

614
00:38:17,929 --> 00:38:20,660
was questioning you
about the accident

615
00:38:20,832 --> 00:38:22,824
and the evidence
that incriminated you,

616
00:38:23,001 --> 00:38:27,166
you paid him to bury that evidence
and not report what he had found?

617
00:38:27,338 --> 00:38:28,499
I did no such thing.

618
00:38:28,673 --> 00:38:31,802
Isn't that why you and your wife
had such a bitter argument that night?

619
00:38:31,976 --> 00:38:34,070
- We didn't argue.
- What did she threaten you with?

620
00:38:34,245 --> 00:38:36,205
To call the police? Want
you to call the Kelloggs?

621
00:38:36,347 --> 00:38:37,987
- What'd she threaten you with?
- Nothing.

622
00:38:38,116 --> 00:38:40,813
She told you she was going to
the police about the hit-and-run

623
00:38:40,985 --> 00:38:42,225
and you killed her, didn't you?

624
00:38:42,387 --> 00:38:45,118
Objection, Your Honor.
No foundation. Speculation.

625
00:38:45,290 --> 00:38:46,451
It's pure fantasy.

626
00:38:46,624 --> 00:38:50,322
Your Honor, I'm just
trying to establish motive.

627
00:38:50,495 --> 00:38:53,522
You either establish
motive or you don't.

628
00:38:53,698 --> 00:38:56,167
As far as I'm concerned,
you've established nothing.

629
00:38:56,768 --> 00:39:00,762
Now, either ask some questions
that are based on the evidence,

630
00:39:00,939 --> 00:39:05,468
and let this man answer
them, or else sit down.

631
00:39:07,745 --> 00:39:08,804
Yes, Your Honor.

632
00:39:11,115 --> 00:39:12,743
Uh...

633
00:39:13,585 --> 00:39:15,986
Where were you when your wife
was killed? Do you remember?

634
00:39:16,154 --> 00:39:19,647
I was at the 50th birthday of
my good friend, Arnold Kruck.

635
00:39:19,824 --> 00:39:23,090
- I got there about 6 and left about 8.
- Mm-hm.

636
00:39:23,261 --> 00:39:24,524
Your wife wasn't invited?

637
00:39:24,696 --> 00:39:25,925
Of course she was.

638
00:39:26,097 --> 00:39:29,090
She wasn't feeling well,
so she stayed home.

639
00:39:29,267 --> 00:39:32,499
Oh, so you went home
before you went to the party?

640
00:39:32,670 --> 00:39:34,332
No, I went to the
party from the office.

641
00:39:34,505 --> 00:39:37,266
Told me she was sick when I talked
to her on the phone that afternoon.

642
00:39:37,442 --> 00:39:40,105
Hmm. Would, uh, ahem...?

643
00:39:41,246 --> 00:39:45,479
Would you please tell the court

644
00:39:46,084 --> 00:39:47,882
when this picture was taken?

645
00:39:49,387 --> 00:39:50,446
Well, that's Arnold Kruck.

646
00:39:51,122 --> 00:39:53,751
It was taken the night
of his 50th birthday party.

647
00:39:53,925 --> 00:39:55,791
His wife Mary, I
believe, took it.

648
00:39:56,561 --> 00:40:02,432
We had this picture blown up
so we could see what time it was.

649
00:40:02,600 --> 00:40:04,193
See that watch right there?

650
00:40:04,369 --> 00:40:06,304
Now, you see your watch?

651
00:40:06,471 --> 00:40:09,031
Tell us what time
that watch says.

652
00:40:09,207 --> 00:40:10,800
Six-twenty.

653
00:40:10,975 --> 00:40:14,070
My wife Brenda
was killed about 6:30,

654
00:40:14,245 --> 00:40:16,908
so as you can see, I
couldn't have been involved.

655
00:40:17,081 --> 00:40:19,710
Yeah, that's what
we thought at first.

656
00:40:19,884 --> 00:40:21,546
But then...

657
00:40:21,886 --> 00:40:24,515
Then we saw something
reflected in the mirror right there.

658
00:40:24,689 --> 00:40:27,818
Can you see that right there?
What is that? What is that?

659
00:40:27,992 --> 00:40:29,790
Looks like a half-eaten
birthday cake.

660
00:40:29,961 --> 00:40:31,156
That's right.

661
00:40:31,596 --> 00:40:33,155
According to the Krucks,

662
00:40:33,331 --> 00:40:36,631
they served dinner that
night promptly at 7 p.m.,

663
00:40:36,801 --> 00:40:40,431
and they served the
cake promptly at 7:45.

664
00:40:41,572 --> 00:40:45,304
And that means that this picture
had to be taken sometime after that.

665
00:40:46,411 --> 00:40:47,606
Oh, I guess my watch stopped.

666
00:40:47,779 --> 00:40:53,116
Maybe. Or maybe you
turned your watch back to 6:20

667
00:40:53,284 --> 00:40:56,982
to give yourself an alibi in case
you ever came under suspicion.

668
00:40:57,655 --> 00:41:00,591
There you are. Ahem.

669
00:41:01,726 --> 00:41:03,888
Now, you wanna
just tell the court

670
00:41:04,062 --> 00:41:05,860
what really happened
that night, Mr. Chaney?

671
00:41:07,265 --> 00:41:09,530
- I already have.
- I bet your wife didn't tell you

672
00:41:09,701 --> 00:41:11,670
she was sick when she called.

673
00:41:11,836 --> 00:41:13,134
I bet she told you

674
00:41:13,304 --> 00:41:16,536
that she was gonna go to the
police about that hit-and-run,

675
00:41:16,708 --> 00:41:18,142
and you came home, didn't you?

676
00:41:18,309 --> 00:41:20,744
- I already told you...
- You argued with her and begged her,

677
00:41:20,912 --> 00:41:24,041
and then something totally
unexpected happened.

678
00:41:24,215 --> 00:41:27,151
The defendant, Clarence
Ryder, broke into your house.

679
00:41:28,453 --> 00:41:29,853
I was at the party.

680
00:41:30,321 --> 00:41:33,257
No. You were at home.

681
00:41:34,258 --> 00:41:37,717
<i>I don't really know
exactly what happened,</i>

682
00:41:37,895 --> 00:41:43,129
<i>but I figure that while Clarence
Ryder was burglarizing your house,</i>

683
00:41:43,301 --> 00:41:46,601
<i>and heard something upstairs,
you heard him downstairs.</i>

684
00:41:46,771 --> 00:41:49,570
<i>And you went down and
saw him leaving the house.</i>

685
00:41:50,041 --> 00:41:52,704
<i>So you grabbed the
letter opener off your desk,</i>

686
00:41:52,877 --> 00:41:55,540
<i>went back up the stairs,
stabbed your wife to death,</i>

687
00:41:55,713 --> 00:41:58,410
<i>knowing the burglar
would be blamed for it,</i>

688
00:41:58,583 --> 00:41:59,812
<i>setoff the alarm,</i>

689
00:41:59,984 --> 00:42:03,853
<i>and then rushed over to the
party to give yourself an alibi.</i>

690
00:42:04,022 --> 00:42:06,514
That's not true. I was at
the party the whole time.

691
00:42:06,691 --> 00:42:08,853
I didn't get home until
after my wife was dead.

692
00:42:09,027 --> 00:42:10,825
Yes, you did.

693
00:42:10,995 --> 00:42:15,194
And the best part of it is,
Your Honor, I can prove it.

694
00:42:16,167 --> 00:42:18,159
You had a meeting
that afternoon at 2:00

695
00:42:18,336 --> 00:42:20,567
with Beachwood Advertising.
You remember that?

696
00:42:21,105 --> 00:42:23,074
Yeah, we signed a
three-year contract with them.

697
00:42:23,241 --> 00:42:25,801
Now, what did they give you
as a memento of that occasion?

698
00:42:25,977 --> 00:42:27,468
You remember?

699
00:42:27,645 --> 00:42:29,011
Gold fountain pen.

700
00:42:30,048 --> 00:42:31,778
Okay.

701
00:42:31,983 --> 00:42:35,420
This is a list of the items

702
00:42:36,087 --> 00:42:39,888
that Mr. Ryder had in his possession
when the police arrested him,

703
00:42:40,058 --> 00:42:41,117
which you later identified

704
00:42:41,292 --> 00:42:43,625
as having been
stolen from your house.

705
00:42:43,795 --> 00:42:47,698
Would you please
read off item number 11?

706
00:42:50,435 --> 00:42:53,269
"One gold fountain pen

707
00:42:53,438 --> 00:42:59,139
with the engraved inscription
Beachwood Advertising Company."

708
00:43:00,545 --> 00:43:02,173
That's right.

709
00:43:03,114 --> 00:43:08,018
Now, how could a pen, that
you had just gotten that afternoon,

710
00:43:08,252 --> 00:43:12,121
wind up being stolen
from your house

711
00:43:12,290 --> 00:43:15,920
if you went directly from the
office to the party like you said?

712
00:43:20,431 --> 00:43:21,922
Well...

713
00:43:22,567 --> 00:43:26,095
I guess we've established
reasonable doubt

714
00:43:26,270 --> 00:43:30,002
that Clarence Ryder
murdered your wife.

715
00:43:30,475 --> 00:43:33,377
That means the police
will reopen the case.

716
00:43:33,544 --> 00:43:35,104
And I imagine you and
Detective Van Owen

717
00:43:35,246 --> 00:43:40,014
will have a long time to
talk about what went wrong.

718
00:43:40,184 --> 00:43:42,813
Because the way I see it,

719
00:43:42,987 --> 00:43:45,479
you're gonna wind
up in the same place.

720
00:43:57,335 --> 00:43:59,065
- Hi.
- Hi.

721
00:43:59,237 --> 00:44:01,069
Where are Jerri and Cliff?

722
00:44:01,239 --> 00:44:05,142
They'll be here.
I told them 6:45.

723
00:44:05,309 --> 00:44:06,675
How come?

724
00:44:06,844 --> 00:44:08,312
Well...

725
00:44:08,813 --> 00:44:13,013
I thought it would...
It would give us time

726
00:44:13,184 --> 00:44:17,781
to talk about this "you
and me" business.

727
00:44:20,791 --> 00:44:22,987
Oh. Julie, um...

728
00:44:25,830 --> 00:44:27,628
That's all right, Ben.

729
00:44:28,199 --> 00:44:30,065
I'm not getting married.

730
00:44:31,235 --> 00:44:32,635
- You're not?
- No.

731
00:44:32,803 --> 00:44:35,534
Well, not at the moment, anyway.

732
00:44:35,706 --> 00:44:38,676
I think I should take
some more time.

733
00:44:39,177 --> 00:44:41,942
I'm very fond of Mike.

734
00:44:42,113 --> 00:44:45,481
I mean, I must be
to consider marriage.

735
00:44:45,716 --> 00:44:47,810
But not just yet.

736
00:44:49,053 --> 00:44:53,423
Coming here and seeing you, uh,

737
00:44:54,625 --> 00:44:58,585
working with you,
I'm just not ready yet.

738
00:45:00,765 --> 00:45:04,600
But what were you going to say?

739
00:45:06,637 --> 00:45:08,196
Well, um...

740
00:45:10,775 --> 00:45:15,236
When I heard you were
coming, I was just tickled to death.

741
